  chapterSummary: "India has a remarkable variety of relief. The major physiographic divisions are the Himalayan Mountains, Northern Plains, Peninsular Plateau, Indian Desert, Coastal Plains and Islands. These landforms are connected with geological history and influence drainage, climate, soils, agriculture, settlements, transport, resources and hazards. India's broad physiographic divisions are based on landform, relief and geological structure rather than political boundaries. The Himalayas are young and tectonically active mountains; the Northern Plains are extensive alluvial deposits; the Peninsular Plateau is an ancient stable block; the Indian Desert is arid; the Coastal Plains occur between plateau and sea; and the island groups form distinct maritime environments. The Himalayas are a young fold mountain system along northern India. They contain very high peaks, glaciers, deep valleys and steep slopes. The Greater Himalaya or Himadri is the highest range, the Lesser Himalaya or Himachal contains varied mountain landscapes and valleys, and the Shiwaliks form the southern foothills. The Himalayas influence climate, drainage and settlement and are also exposed to earthquakes, landslides and other hazards. The Northern Plains were built mainly by alluvium deposited by the Indus, Ganga and Brahmaputra river systems and their tributaries. Their level relief and fertile soils support intensive agriculture, dense settlements and transport. Surface features include the Bhabar, Terai, Bhangar and Khadar. The same river processes that create fertile land can also produce floods, erosion and channel changes. The Peninsular Plateau is one of India's oldest landmasses and is composed largely of ancient crystalline rocks. The Central Highlands lie north of the Narmada while the Deccan Plateau lies south of it. The Western Ghats form a prominent western edge and the Eastern Ghats are more discontinuous. Plateau landscapes are important for minerals, forests, agriculture, waterfalls and rivers. The Indian Desert, especially the Thar region, lies mainly in western Rajasthan. It receives low and variable rainfall and contains sand dunes, sandy plains and seasonal drainage. Thorny and drought-resistant vegetation is common. People depend on livestock, irrigation, water conservation, trade and tourism. Modern development has changed parts of the region but water remains a critical limiting factor. The western coastal plain is generally narrow and includes the Konkan, Kannad and Malabar sections. The eastern coastal plain is generally broader and includes the Northern Circar and Coromandel Coast. Large east-flowing rivers form deltas on the eastern side, while estuaries and backwaters are important in several western coastal areas. Coasts support ports, fisheries, agriculture and tourism but face cyclones, erosion and storm surges. Lakshadweep in the Arabian Sea and the Andaman and Nicobar Islands in the Bay of Bengal form India's major island groups. Lakshadweep is strongly associated with coral environments, while the Andaman and Nicobar group contains hilly islands and varied ecosystems. Islands are important for biodiversity, fisheries, tourism and maritime geography but are environmentally sensitive. Relief shapes human activity without determining it completely. Mountains can support horticulture, forestry, hydropower and tourism but make transport difficult. Plains support intensive agriculture and dense settlement. Plateaus provide mineral resources and varied farming conditions. Deserts favour drought-adapted livelihoods, while coasts support ports, fisheries and tourism. Understanding these relationships is central to physical geography.",
